     No, there is no strategy involved in the issue of confirmation. 
Would you say that it is a matter of strategy not to make such mistakes
of manual dexterity, or skill?
     I will say that many, quite probably most, of such occurances are
mistakes, not line noise, and for the most part not due to slow systems.
But I still think the issue is not interface mistake vs. user mistake,
but rather skill vs. strategy.
     Skill and strategy both require thought.  However, the primary
focus of thought in a skill-oriented game is where one is going to move
one's hand next.  In a strategy-oriented game, it focusses on the
ramifications of picking a certain option.  Now, it doesn't take a whole
lot of thought to conclude that attacking a peaceful monster, or falling
in a pool, or so forth, would be a bad idea.
     My solution is to remove coaligned monsters from the game
altogether, since they add little or no strategic elements, and to
completely eliminate the idea of stepping into a pool (i.e. just like a
wall, but you can see through it).  Needless to say, this doesn't have
universal support, but as far as my logic on the game goes, it makes
sense. 

